# Installed feed package directories to remove again, as <feed>/<dir>.
# scripts/feeds keys source packages by directory name, so two feeds carrying
# the same binary package under different directories both get installed.

# luci_smstool carries its own LuCI app, which would collide with the
# packages feed's sms-tool-js page. Only sms-tool itself is wanted.
luci_smstool/luci-app-sms-tool-js

# dotycat's luci-app-nft-ttl writes its own /etc/nftables.d/ttl64.nft and a
# postrouting rule keyed on iifname br-lan. byte-ttl already owns the modem
# egress rewrite (and does it on the output interface, which is the correct
# hook for "everything leaving through the modem"); installing both means two
# rules rewriting the same packets, and the LuCI page would silently disagree
# with the running config.
#
# Note: this package is from dotycat, not from XT-Martinez - XT's aw1000-feed
# ships no TTL handling at all. It is excluded because byte-ttl is the
# superset (TTL, HopLimit, per-family, wwan-only or global), not because of
# where it comes from. To use the LuCI page instead, drop this line, remove
# byte-ttl from the overlay, and set nftttl.ttl.enabled=1.
dotycat/luci-app-nft-ttl

# The nss feed's wwan/app/ tree ships its own modem managers and LuCI pages:
#   luci-app-usbmodem   depends on kmod-qmi_wwan_q, which quectel-cm selects,
#                       which makes Kconfig report a recursive dependency
#   luci-app-pcimodem   the PCIe-MHI sibling, same stack
#   luci-proto-quectel  this repo builds its own (wwan/luci-proto-quectel)
# None of them are wanted: the modem UI here is luci-app-aw1000-modem, and the
# protocol form comes from wwan/. Excluding the directories keeps `feeds
# install -a` from pulling them in.
nss/luci-app-usbmodem
nss/luci-app-pcimodem
nss/luci-proto-quectel
